INT. DRAINED ROLLER RINK - TEMPORARY COUNTY COURTROOM - DAY

A roller rink. Maple floor, warped by flood. Disco ball still hangs over the bench, wrapped in bubble wrap.

Folding tables. Folding chairs. A flag on a mic stand.

Courthouse is under repair. Justice is here instead.

ADAEZE OKAFOR (40s, sharp navy suit, sneakers under the table, reading glasses pushed into cropped grey hair) stands at the defense table. Alone. Her client's chair is empty.

Across the aisle: LEV BRANDT (50s, heavy, rumpled, a pastry crumb on his tie) shuffles exhibits. Calm. Too calm.

On a raised DJ booth, now a bench: JUDGE HOLLIS TRAN (60s, silver braid over one shoulder, half-moon spectacles, a thermos of tea). She taps a gavel made from a skate wheel and a dowel.

Rink speakers hiss.

JUDGE TRAN
Back on the record.

LEV
Your Honor, the People are ready.

ADAEZE
The defense is not.

JUDGE TRAN
Counsel, your client is late.

ADAEZE
My client is in a hospital.

LEV
Convenient.

ADAEZE
Say that again.

LEV
I said, convenient.

JUDGE TRAN
Mr. Brandt.

LEV
It is, Judge. Day three of trial. Day three of a medical emergency.

ADAEZE
He had a seizure on the stairs.

LEV
Which stairs?

ADAEZE
The stairs of this building. The one with the leak.

A drip. Somewhere. Plink. Plink.

Everyone listens.

JUDGE TRAN
I have a doctor's note?

ADAEZE
I filed it at seven.

LEV
I never received it.

ADAEZE
I emailed you.

LEV
My inbox says otherwise.

ADAEZE
Your inbox says a lot of things.

JUDGE TRAN
Enough.

Silence. The disco ball turns a quarter inch in a draft.

Lev smiles. Opens a folder. Slides one page onto the table.

LEV
Then the People move to proceed in absentia.

ADAEZE
Objection.

JUDGE TRAN
Basis?

ADAEZE
Every basis.

LEV
She can't list one.

ADAEZE
Sixth Amendment. Confrontation. Presence. Counsel of choice.

LEV
He waived presence on Monday.

ADAEZE
He did not.

LEV
Page four of the waiver.

Adaeze freezes. Hands go to her own stack. Flips. Flips.

JUDGE TRAN
Ms. Okafor?

ADAEZE
Page four.

She finds it. Reads. Her jaw sets.

A signature. Her client's. Dated Monday.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
That is not his hand.

LEV
It is his hand.

ADAEZE
He signs with a loop. This has no loop.

LEV
People change.

ADAEZE
Not in a week.

JUDGE TRAN
Counsel, approach.

They do. Skates of silence. Their shoes squeak on the maple.

JUDGE TRAN (CONT'D)
(low)
Mr. Brandt. Where did you get this?

LEV
(low)
From the clerk.

ADAEZE
(low)
Which clerk?

LEV
The one who works here.

ADAEZE
There are four.

LEV
The one with the lanyard.

ADAEZE
They all have lanyards!

JUDGE TRAN
Lower your voice.

ADAEZE
(hissing)
You forged it.

LEV
Careful.

ADAEZE
Or you let someone forge it. Same thing.

LEV
That is slander, counselor.

JUDGE TRAN
Stop. Both of you.

Beat. The drip: plink.

Judge Tran lifts the page toward the light. Tilts it. Squints.

Her thumb rubs the signature. Black comes away. Wet.

She holds the thumb up for them to see.

JUDGE TRAN (CONT'D)
Ink's still wet.

Lev's smile dies.

LEV
Your Honor, that's - that can't -

ADAEZE
Monday, you said.

LEV
The photocopier smears.

JUDGE TRAN
It is a pen, Mr. Brandt. A felt-tip. Not a copier.

LEV
I can explain.

ADAEZE
Please. Do.

LEV
It was a... a re-signing. For the file.

JUDGE TRAN
By whom?

Lev opens his mouth. Closes it.

Behind him, the rink doors swing open. Wind. A clerk in a lanyard stands there, small, pale, holding a felt-tip pen like a dropped weapon.

CLERK (O.S.)
I'm sorry. He said it was routine.

Nobody moves.

JUDGE TRAN
(quiet)
Mr. Brandt. Sit down.

Lev sits. Slowly. His chair squeaks on the maple.

ADAEZE
Your Honor, the defense moves for dismissal with prejudice.

JUDGE TRAN
Granted.

The skate-wheel gavel. One tap. Echo, bright against the rafters.

Adaeze exhales. Takes off her glasses. Rubs her eyes.

ADAEZE
(to herself)
Sneakers. Best decision I made all week.

Judge Tran pours tea. Steam curls in the cold.

JUDGE TRAN
Court is adjourned. Somebody turn that ball off.

The disco ball stops turning.
